Rachel: quick hitI pushed a Terraform change that updates a per-model ConfigMap but the running pods didn't pick up the new config. they mount the file under /etc/model-config. TF snippet I used:
```
resource "kubernetes_config_map" "model_x_cfg" {
  metadata { name = "model-x-config" }
  data = {
    "config.yaml" = file("../configs/model-x.yaml")
  }
}
```
kubectl shows the new CM data but app still sees old values. is there a recommended pattern we standardize on?

Noah: k8s won't restart pods when a ConfigMap changes. if it's mounted as a volume the kubelet eventually updates the file in-place, but apps need to either watch the file or you need to change the pod template so kube does a rolling update. also beware projected volumes are "eventually consistent" so your app should tolerate transient partial writes.

Li: common infra pattern: compute a checksum of the config in TF and inject it into the Deployment's pod template annotations so any change updates the pod template hash and triggers a rollout. TF example (sha256 around file):
```
locals {
  cfg = file("../configs/model-x.yaml")
  cfg_hash = sha256(local.cfg)
}
resource "kubernetes_deployment" "model_x" {
  metadata { name = "model-x" }
  spec {
    template {
      metadata {
        annotations = {
          "infra.redwood/config-sha" = local.cfg_hash
        }
      }
      # containers ...
    }
  }
}
```
That forces the rollout and is CI-friendly.

Omar: tradeoffs at scale: we have hundreds of model deployments. rolling each one on frequent tweaks is noisy. alternatives we've used: aggregate non-sensitive knobs into a single ConfigMap keyed by model id, or run a small sidecar/operator that watches a central store and pushes config updates into the process (no pod restart). both add complexity.

Kate: if you're templating with Helm you can template a checksum into deployment.yaml using a helper that computes sha256 of the file. kustomize users can use a generator or a small plugin that updates annotations similarly.

devops-bot: configmap "model-x-config" updated by Rachel (terraform). no rollout triggered.

Rachel: thanks. i'll add locals.cfg_hash + pod template annotation in the TF PR and include a one-line note about hot-reload caveats. planning to add a smoke e2e: update config -> wait for rollout -> sanity-check model endpoint. PTAL when I open the PR later. :thumbsup:

Li: +1 on the e2e. also add a small backoff in the app when parsing the file to avoid failing on partial writes.

Omar: one more gotcha: double-check the CI artifact used by TF (the file read by sha256) matches what the cluster will receive if you have separate pipelines. mismatches cause annoying drift.

Kate: quick manual test you can run locally: `kubectl create configmap --from-file=../configs/model-x.yaml -o yaml --dry-run=client | kubectl apply -f -` then `kubectl rollout status deploy/model-x -n inference` and `kubectl exec -n inference $(kubectl get pods -l app=model-x -o jsonpath='{.items[0].metadata.name}') -- cat /etc/model-config/config.yaml` to verify the mounted file.

Rachel: will do. pinging reviewers: @Li @Noah @Omar @Kate please PTAL on the TF PR when it's up.
